<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Mainz</h1><p class="st-meta">1793 CE – 1793 CE · capital Mainz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first democratic republic on German soil, a short experiment under French protection in revolutionary Mainz.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>French troops under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-philippe-comte-de-custine">Custine</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> in October 1792, and the elector fled. A Jacobin club formed, and in early 1793 elections were held under French supervision. On 18 March 1793 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhenish-german-national-convention">Rhenish-German National Convention</a> proclaimed a free state covering the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landau">Landau</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bingen-am-rhein">Bingen</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The convention, led by the professor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andreas-joseph-hofmann">Andreas Joseph Hofmann</a> and the naturalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-forster">Georg Forster</a>, abolished feudal rule. Many inhabitants refused to vote or swear oaths, and the republic had no real authority beyond the French army's reach. On 21 March the convention asked to be annexed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and Forster went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to present the request.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Prussian and Austrian forces besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, which surrendered in July 1793, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobin-club">Jacobins</a> were persecuted. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-forster">Forster</a> died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1794. The left bank later passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the republic is remembered as an early German democracy.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-mainz.jpg" alt="Republic of Mainz" width="320" height="535"><figcaption>Image: Kamée, upload von Martin Bahmann · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Freiheitsbaum-Mainz-1793.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1793 CE – 1793 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mainz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Andreas Joseph Hofmann, Georg Forster</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rhenish-German Free State, Mainzer Republik</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1793&amp;focus=republic-of-mainz" aria-label="Republic of Mainz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4870 438 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Mainz lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5190 694L5190 694M5190 694L5054 728M5190 694L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5190 694h0M5054 728h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5190 694h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5190 694h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1793&amp;focus=republic-of-mainz">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
